# Plan — etude-9uf.4: Shrink the completeness audit to its two live ratchets

## What is being replaced

| File | Lines |
|---|---|
| `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit.sh` | 902 |
| `scripts/dogfood-completeness-audit_test.sh` | 1579 |
| **total** | **2481** |

Replaced by one script of roughly 60 lines enforcing two checks, plus a focused
test.

## Why these two survive and the rest do not

The epic's premise: the completeness audit exists because stage records were
RECONSTRUCTED after the fact, so something had to police whether the
reconstruction had happened. Under the supervised model each phase is captured
and gated as it completes, so most of what the audit checks is either
structurally true or self-imposed bookkeeping.

**Kept:**

- **(d) refs pushed** — every `refs/etude/{runs,retros}/*` matches origin. This
  is the one check that catches a genuine, silent, unrecoverable loss: an
  unpushed run ref dies with the worktree. It caught real misses, and it caught
  one during bead `.3`.
- **(c) retro cadence** — a retro every 3 closed beads. Self-imposed, but it is
  the mechanism by which this lane learns anything, and it does not become
  structurally true under the new model. **Kept WARN-only, exactly as it is
  today** — see below.

**Dropped:**

| Check | Why |
|---|---|
| (e) docs drift | duplicates `make docs-check` / `make docs-reality`, which the verify gate already runs as deterministic checks. WARN-only today, so it blocks nothing. |
| (f) cadence-sidecar | the 7-key sidecar is bookkeeping about retros, not evidence about work. **See the consequence below — this one is not free.** |
| bypass REPORT | the printed list of allowlisted beads. The allowlist FILE stays (see below); only the informational report goes. |
| (g) retro subject consistency | WARN-only; checks that a retro's title id-list matches its manifest refs. Bookkeeping about bookkeeping. |
| `--bead` and `--since` modes | `--bead` was the per-close mode, and its only caller was `scripts/dogfood-close.sh`, deleted in bead `.3`. Nothing in the tree invokes either now except the audit's own tests. |

## The bead's premise for dropping check (a) does not hold

**This is the most important thing in this plan and it needs a decision before
implementation.**

The bead says checks (a) and (b) "become structurally true once `etude gate` is
the only way to advance a phase". For (b) that holds. For (a) it does not,
because **nothing makes `etude gate` the only way to advance a phase.** Verified:
no hook, no CLI path, and no `bd close` guard requires a run ref or a gate before
a bead closes. The pre-push hook blocks code pushes on audit gaps — and check (a)
IS the audit gap that fires. It is the only detector of a bead closed with no run
ref at all, i.e. a supervisor who skipped the model entirely.

Check (d) does not cover this: it compares local refs against origin, so a bead
with no run ref has nothing to compare and passes silently.

So dropping (a) does not remove a check made redundant by the new model. It
removes the only thing that notices the new model was not used. The epic's own
complaint about the old world was "2,481 lines of audit policing a skippable
manual protocol"; deleting the policing while the protocol stays skippable keeps
the worse half of that trade.

### And the same argument saves (b)

An earlier draft of this plan kept (a) on this reasoning and then dropped (b) on
the grounds that "a run that has a ref and advanced through `etude gate` has
gates, because gating is how it advanced". The plan gate called that a tautology,
and it is: it assumes the gating the previous paragraph just showed is not
enforced.

Verified: `internal/cli/capture.go` writes `refs/etude/runs/*` independently of
gating, and `runmanifest` marshals `gates,omitempty`, so a captured-but-never-
gated run is a VALID manifest with zero gates. That state is exactly as reachable
as the never-captured one, and check (b) is its only detector. A supervisor who
captures each phase and never gates any of them produces a run that (a) and (d)
both pass.

**Recommendation: keep (a) AND (b).** Together they are about ten lines, and they
are the difference between a model that is followed and a model that merely
exists. Concretely: keep (a), (b), (c), (d); drop (e), (f), (g) and the bypass
report.

This contradicts the bead's acceptance, which names two ratchets, so it is raised
rather than decided unilaterally. If the plan gate agrees, the bead text is
updated in this bead with a note, as `etude-1od` was. Note the shrink is barely
affected: 2481 lines become well under a hundred either way. The bead's real goal
was never "exactly two checks", it was "small enough to read in one screen".

### The allowlist stays, because the bead is wrong about what it serves

The bead says the allowlist "goes with the checks it served", meaning (a) and
(b). It also feeds check (c): `active_ids` is `in_scope_ids` minus the
allowlisted beads, and (c) counts uncovered beads by iterating `active_ids`. The
allowlist is therefore the cadence denominator.

**Measured, not assumed.** With the allowlist emptied and the current `--last 9`
window, check (c)'s uncovered count goes from **1 to 3** — exactly the `>= 3`
threshold. So deleting the allowlist while also making (c) hard would make
`make dogfood-audit` exit 1 on a clean tree and block every push in this
checkout: the plan's own top-listed risk, triggered by the plan's own change.
The plan gate predicted this precisely and the prediction was confirmed by
running it.

So the allowlist file is kept as-is and all surviving checks respect it exactly
as they do today. It is data, not code — 38 non-comment entries — and it does not
count against "small enough to read in one screen", which is a claim about the
script. (The earlier figure of 43 counted blank lines; the gate caught that too.)

## Two consequences that are NOT free, stated up front

### 1. Dropping (f) leaves a live consumer reading an unenforced convention

`scripts/retro-meta-index.sh` is a surviving, working tool that aggregates the
7-key retro-meta sidecars across cadence retros. Check (f) is what guarantees
post-cutoff cadence retros carry a valid sidecar. Drop (f) and the index keeps
working on what exists, but nothing ensures new retros contribute — so it
degrades silently as the lane continues.

That is precisely the failure bead `.3` spent nine gate rounds on: a mechanism
left pointing at something no longer guaranteed. Options:

- **(i) Drop (f) and accept the degradation**, documenting in `docs/retro.md`
  and the index's own header that sidecars are now best-effort, and filing a bead
  if the index is to be kept honest.
- **(ii) Keep a minimal sidecar assertion** inside the new script.
- **(iii) Drop (f) and move the assertion into `retro-meta-index.sh`**, where the
  consumer validates its own input.

**Chosen: (i), with the documentation and the follow-on ALREADY FILED as `etude-k47`** —
filed during planning rather than promised, because an unfiled follow-on is the
same "mechanism pointing at something no longer guaranteed" this option is
about. The bead's
acceptance names exactly two ratchets and says the rest are dropped; smuggling a
third in under a different name would be reinterpreting the acceptance, which is
the failure mode this epic exists to end. (iii) is a real improvement but it is
work on the index, not on the audit, and belongs in its own bead. What is NOT
acceptable is dropping (f) silently and leaving `docs/retro.md` still saying the
convention is "enforced by check (f)".

### 2. The cadence check counts retro REFS, not retro markdown

Check (c) walks `refs/etude/retros/*` for manifests whose `refs.trigger` is
`cadence-retro`, and counts closed beads not covered by one. But this lane's
operating instruction is that retros are markdown committed under
`.etude/retros/`, and the three retros written on this epic are markdown; only
two cadence retro REFS exist for it (`etude-9uf.2`, `etude-rep`).

So the ratchet and the practice measure different things, and the ratchet is
currently passing only because it is under its threshold (1 uncovered < 3).

**Chosen: keep the ref-based check unchanged in what it measures.** Reasons: the
refs are the durable, pushable artifact the audit's sibling check (d) already
guards; markdown under `.etude/retros/` is committed to the branch and is
already protected by ordinary code review; and changing what a ratchet measures
inside a bead whose job is to SHRINK it mixes two changes. The gap is real, is
documented in the new script's header, and is filed as `etude-3xt`. It is recorded as a
known limitation rather than quietly resolved.

### Check (c) stays WARN, and the acceptance is met without it

An earlier draft of this plan asserted both that the exit-code contract was
"preserved exactly" and that a negative control would show (c) exiting 1. Those
cannot both be true: today (c) calls `log_warn`, only `gap_count` drives the exit
code, and (c) therefore blocks nothing. The plan gate caught the contradiction.

Resolved in favour of WARN, for a reason beyond consistency: **(c) measures retro
REFS while this lane's actual practice is retro MARKDOWN.** Making a ratchet hard
on the shared push path is a tightening that must be justified; making one hard
when it measures the wrong artifact is worse than leaving it as a signal. A
ratchet that fires on the wrong evidence trains people to bypass it.

So the acceptance's "exits non-zero on a real gap" is satisfied by (a), (b) and
(d) — three hard checks — with (c) retained as the warning it already is. That is
stated plainly rather than letting "two ratchets" imply two hard ones.

## Contract that must not change

The pre-push hook (`.beads/hooks/pre-push`) branches on the audit's exit code and
**fails closed on anything unexpected**:

- `0` — clean, push allowed
- `1` — hard gaps, push BLOCKED with the GAP lines shown
- `2` — usage / environment error, push BLOCKED (fail-closed branch)

The new script preserves all three exactly. The hook also honours
`DOGFOOD_HOOK_AUDIT_SCRIPT` for injection, and `scripts/pre-push_test.sh`
(extracted in `.3`) drives that path in 10 of its 20 cases — including B9
(missing audit script) and B10 (audit exits 2 → hook fails closed). **Those 20
cases must stay green**, and they are the real regression net for this bead.

`--last <N>` must keep working: the hook invokes `--last 9`, and `make
dogfood-audit` invokes `--last 9`.

